The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ctl & DMA_CTL_STATUS_OWN)
    break;
```
```c
if (handled < weight) {
    napi_complete_done(napi, handled);
    bcm4908_enet_intrs_on(enet);
}
```
Reason:
The first statement checks if the descriptor is owned by the hardware, and if so, it breaks out of the loop, potentially leaving the RX ring disabled if the hardware runs out of free descriptors. The second statement completes the NAPI polling without re-enabling the RX ring, which can lead to a situation where incoming packets are not processed, especially under high load.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bcm4908_enet_dma_rx_ring_enable(enet, &enet->rx_ring);
```
Reason:
This statement is added to re-enable the RX ring after processing the received packets. By ensuring that the RX ring is enabled every time after handling packets, it prevents the hardware from stopping the reception of packets due to a lack of free descriptors, thus allowing the driver to continue processing incoming packets.

